WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.127
You can't tell
from looking at me,

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:04.629
but I actually grew up
deep in the woods.

00:00:04.713 --> 00:00:07.298
My childhood home was
an antiquated log cabin

00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:08.967
surrounded by so much forest,

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:11.761
you couldn't even see
the distant road in the winter.

00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:14.681
So I spent my free time
playing among the trees,

00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:15.890
climbing their branches,

00:00:15.974 --> 00:00:17.642
and seeing what treasures
I could discover

00:00:17.726 --> 00:00:18.977
among their roots.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.688
We relied on wood from those
trees to warm our house,

00:00:21.771 --> 00:00:22.939
cook our meals,
and heat up water.

00:00:23.022 --> 00:00:26.151
Some provided
food, others syrup,

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:27.902
and they all provided shade.

00:00:27.986 --> 00:00:30.530
That childhood, while
certainly imperfect,

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:32.991
gave me a deep
appreciation for nature.

00:00:33.074 --> 00:00:35.660
I was so grateful for
everything the trees provided

00:00:35.744 --> 00:00:38.496
that I did the 21st century
equivalent of begging Gaia

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:39.956
to turn me into a dryad

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:41.958
and got a tattoo of
an oak tree on my arm.

00:00:42.041 --> 00:00:43.960
But I was by no means
the first person

00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:47.338
to pay my respects
to the majestic tree.

00:00:47.422 --> 00:00:49.090
Humans around the world
have been doing that

00:00:49.174 --> 00:00:50.633
for thousands of years

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:52.844
in ways that were
immortalized in our myths.

00:00:52.927 --> 00:00:55.972
[light upbeat music]

00:00:56.848 --> 00:00:59.267
Even without all of the vital,

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:01.269
practical things trees provide,

00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:04.022
it's hard not to see them
as divine or magical.

00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:05.940
Their sheer size
can seem to connect

00:01:06.024 --> 00:01:07.358
the Earth to the heavens,

00:01:07.442 --> 00:01:09.277
and their centuries
long lifespans

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.738
mean a single tree can
watch over a family

00:01:11.821 --> 00:01:14.115
or community for
several generations,

00:01:14.199 --> 00:01:17.076
long enough that its
origin fades into legend.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.288
Even the deciduous trees that
shed their leaves every year

00:01:20.371 --> 00:01:21.790
blossom again in the spring,

00:01:21.873 --> 00:01:23.666
showing that they
weren't dead at all,

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.003
just taking a little nap before
it returned with its gifts.

00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:28.922
It was easy for many
of our ancestors

00:01:29.005 --> 00:01:31.382
to interpret
something so immortal

00:01:31.466 --> 00:01:35.386
and abundant as the gift
or even embodiment of a god.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.222
But I think the
reciprocal nature

00:01:37.305 --> 00:01:38.640
of our relationship to trees

00:01:38.723 --> 00:01:41.684
is the real reason they're
so common in folklore.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:45.104
We rely on them,
but they also rely on us.

00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:48.650
And beneath every story about
the wonders trees provide,

00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:51.277
there's a sometimes
not so hidden message

00:01:51.361 --> 00:01:54.197
about what will happen if
we don't take care of them.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.366
In Thailand,
Buddhist folklore tells

00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:59.494
of a tree called
the Nariphon or Makkaliphon

00:01:59.577 --> 00:02:01.996
that grows fruit in the
shape of young women.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.250
According to one of the
Jataka or a series of texts

00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:07.710
that described the various
lives of the Buddha,

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:09.796
the trees were planted
in a sacred grove

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:12.340
by the god Indra
to protect, Maddi,

00:02:12.423 --> 00:02:14.509
Buddha's wife in
a previous life.

00:02:14.592 --> 00:02:17.053
In that life the Buddha was
known as Prince Vessantara,

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:20.682
the son of a woman who
was beloved by Indra.

00:02:20.765 --> 00:02:22.600
Vessantara was so generous

00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:25.019
that he was exiled
from his own kingdom

00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:27.355
for giving a gift that
he shouldn't have.

00:02:27.438 --> 00:02:30.733
While in exile, Vessantara
and Maddi traveled

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:32.735
through the legendary
Himavanta forest

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:35.113
full of dangerous creatures.

00:02:35.196 --> 00:02:37.866
It's said that Indra shaped
the fruit like a young woman

00:02:37.949 --> 00:02:40.910
to entice ill-intentioned
men who would be drawn

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:45.456
to the Nariphon for obvious
and disgusting reasons.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.543
Thus, they would be
distracted from chasing Maddi.

00:02:48.626 --> 00:02:50.420
If a man picked the
Nariphon's fruit

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:52.547
and took it to his bed, gross,

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:54.591
he would quickly
find himself sterile

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:57.176
and stripped of any
powers he might possess.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.679
Women, however, were
immune to the fruit,

00:02:59.762 --> 00:03:01.180
and instead could use it

00:03:01.264 --> 00:03:03.433
as a fertility talisman if
they waited for the fruit

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:06.519
to fall naturally from the
tree after a week of growing.

00:03:06.603 --> 00:03:09.147
The location of the
legendary Himavanta forest

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:11.900
and the grove of Nariphon
trees is unknown,

00:03:11.983 --> 00:03:13.610
but you can still
find street vendors

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:15.278
in Thailand who claim to sell

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:18.281
these woman shaped dried
fruits to boost fertility.

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:21.659
Just a few years ago, a video
circulating on social media

00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:24.412
showing what appeared to be
one of these Nariphon trees

00:03:24.495 --> 00:03:26.247
got a lot of attention.

00:03:26.331 --> 00:03:28.750
Though the videos were
never verified as real,

00:03:28.833 --> 00:03:30.335
this shows that the myth

00:03:30.418 --> 00:03:33.671
of woman-shaped fruit is alive
and well in Southeast Asia.

00:03:33.755 --> 00:03:36.507
So for some cultures, trees
and their fruits were gifts

00:03:36.591 --> 00:03:38.676
from the gods to us humans.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.219
Other stories positioned trees

00:03:40.303 --> 00:03:43.264
as the botanical embodiment
of a mystical figure.

00:03:43.348 --> 00:03:46.643
For example, the dryads who
helped inspire my tattoo

00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:49.646
were minor Greek goddesses
or nymphs who inhabited

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:52.106
and looked after
trees and woodlands.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:54.859
Dryads served as the
spirits of trees,

00:03:54.943 --> 00:03:57.528
and when they flourished,
their tree thrived.

00:03:57.612 --> 00:04:00.657
When a dryad was sick or
injured, so was their tree.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.117
Originally, the term
dryad only referred

00:04:03.201 --> 00:04:04.953
to the spirits of oak trees,

00:04:05.036 --> 00:04:07.205
which were considered
sacred to Zeus.

00:04:07.288 --> 00:04:09.707
Ash trees were
guarded by Meliae,

00:04:09.791 --> 00:04:11.918
certain mountain
trees by Oreiades,

00:04:12.001 --> 00:04:14.379
and fruit trees by Meliades.

00:04:14.462 --> 00:04:16.839
Laurels were inhabited
by the Daphnaie,

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:18.758
named for the
water nymph Daphne,

00:04:18.841 --> 00:04:22.220
who was unlucky enough to catch
the god Apollo's attention.

00:04:22.303 --> 00:04:24.472
After running from the
sun god's advances,

00:04:24.555 --> 00:04:26.975
Daphne implored Gaia,
the Earth mother, for help.

00:04:27.058 --> 00:04:29.811
She was transformed
into a laurel tree

00:04:29.894 --> 00:04:32.438
and saved from Apollo's
relentless pursuit,

00:04:32.522 --> 00:04:34.357
only for the laurels
she protected

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.859
to become sacred to
the god she fled.

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:40.196
Dryad eventually became the
general term for any tree nymph

00:04:40.279 --> 00:04:42.699
due to the oak trees
prevalence in ancient Greece

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:44.826
and their connection to
the king of the gods.

00:04:44.909 --> 00:04:46.619
As spirits of the forest,

00:04:46.703 --> 00:04:49.998
dryads could make plants
grow or die as they pleased,

00:04:50.081 --> 00:04:52.959
but mostly they acted as
stewards of their woods.

00:04:53.042 --> 00:04:55.503
This idea that trees and
other elements of nature

00:04:55.586 --> 00:04:57.296
each had their own living spirit

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:59.590
certainly wasn't
unique to the Greeks.

00:04:59.674 --> 00:05:00.800
Animism was practiced

00:05:00.883 --> 00:05:03.428
by many of Europe's
pre-Christian pagans,

00:05:03.511 --> 00:05:06.347
like the Celts and Germanic
tribes who respected

00:05:06.431 --> 00:05:09.225
or even worshiped the
spirits of inanimate things.

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:10.810
Those spirits weren't
necessarily

00:05:10.893 --> 00:05:12.520
considered gods, however.

00:05:12.603 --> 00:05:14.063
As far as we can tell,

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:16.649
the Celtic Tree of Life,
or Crann Bethadh,

00:05:16.733 --> 00:05:18.776
was central to Celtic culture,

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:21.696
but it didn't represent
a specific important deity.

00:05:21.779 --> 00:05:25.074
Instead, it represented
the balance of forces in nature.

00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:26.868
The Celts saw a beauty

00:05:26.951 --> 00:05:29.829
in the way that the trees roots
stretched deep below ground

00:05:29.912 --> 00:05:32.790
while its branches stretched
high into the heavens,

00:05:32.874 --> 00:05:33.833
demonstrating the connection

00:05:33.916 --> 00:05:36.461
between different
realms of the cosmos.

00:05:36.544 --> 00:05:39.213
To the Celts, the tree
of life was an oak tree,

00:05:39.297 --> 00:05:41.841
likely because these were
the oldest tallest trees

00:05:41.924 --> 00:05:43.342
in their sacred forests,

00:05:43.426 --> 00:05:45.720
so tall that they would
often be struck by lightning,

00:05:45.803 --> 00:05:47.096
which would be interpreted

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.515
as a sign of their
favor by the gods.

00:05:49.599 --> 00:05:51.809
Plenty of other cultures
had their own tree of life

00:05:51.893 --> 00:05:54.437
that represented the
balance of natural forces,

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:56.647
but some of them also
provided connections

00:05:56.731 --> 00:05:58.149
between different worlds.

00:05:58.232 --> 00:06:01.611
These are often called
world or cosmic trees.

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:03.029
We mentioned the baobab tree

00:06:03.112 --> 00:06:05.323
in our episode on
Yoruba folklore.

00:06:05.406 --> 00:06:06.783
This tree was said
to be the home

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:08.743
of the Orisha in
their heavenly realm

00:06:08.826 --> 00:06:11.746
before Obatala created
land down here on Earth.

00:06:11.829 --> 00:06:14.791
The baobab is often called
the African Tree of Life

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:16.876
because of the way it
looms over the desert,

00:06:16.959 --> 00:06:19.837
providing shade and
hundreds of valuable uses

00:06:19.921 --> 00:06:21.631
with its bark and sap.

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:24.425
Those trees can
live for 3000 years

00:06:24.509 --> 00:06:27.845
and grow over 100 feet
tall and 50 feet wide.

00:06:27.929 --> 00:06:29.806
Many origin myths for the baobab

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:31.974
involve it being
planted upside down.

00:06:32.058 --> 00:06:33.101
In one story, the baobab tree

00:06:33.184 --> 00:06:35.937
kept walking after
it was planted,

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:38.106
so God ripped it up and
replanted it upside down.

00:06:38.189 --> 00:06:41.317
In another, the hyena
was assigned the job

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.570
of planting the baobab
tree, but found it so ugly

00:06:44.654 --> 00:06:47.115
that he planted
its branches in the dirt.

00:06:47.198 --> 00:06:49.784
Another cosmic tree
on the other side of the world

00:06:49.867 --> 00:06:53.412
is the Ceiba, sacred to many
peoples throughout the tropics,

00:06:53.496 --> 00:06:55.456
including the Maya
of Mesoamerica

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:58.459
and the Taino from
Puerto Rico and the Caribbean.

00:06:58.543 --> 00:07:00.545
The Mayans called it Yaxche

00:07:00.628 --> 00:07:01.921
and believed it was a world tree

00:07:02.004 --> 00:07:04.841
that acted as the scaffolding
for their universe.

00:07:04.924 --> 00:07:06.884
The Ceiba trees'
irregular bloom cycle

00:07:06.968 --> 00:07:08.678
demonstrated the unbreakable

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:11.639
and traversable tether
between life and death.

00:07:11.722 --> 00:07:13.724
So it's no surprise
that Yaxche's roots

00:07:13.808 --> 00:07:17.061
and branches served as portals
to the Mayan afterlife.

00:07:17.145 --> 00:07:20.273
Spirits could climb them to
reach their final destinations.

00:07:20.356 --> 00:07:23.317
The Ceiba tree is such an
important cultural symbol

00:07:23.401 --> 00:07:26.863
that when a 400 year old Ceiba
blossomed on Vieques island

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:30.658
after being ravaged by
Hurricanes Irma and Maria,

00:07:30.741 --> 00:07:33.703
the islanders saw it as a sign
of hope and encouragement

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:36.038
that they would bounce
back from all the damage.

00:07:36.122 --> 00:07:38.875
Perhaps the most well-known
world tree for mythology

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:40.459
is the Norse Yggdrasil.

00:07:40.543 --> 00:07:42.044
This cosmic tree
was said to hold

00:07:42.128 --> 00:07:44.255
all the nine worlds
of the Norse cosmos

00:07:44.338 --> 00:07:48.176
in its roots and branches,
including Midgard and Asgard.

00:07:48.259 --> 00:07:51.053
Some sources claim that
Yggdrasil is an ash tree,

00:07:51.137 --> 00:07:52.555
but that's hotly debated.

00:07:52.638 --> 00:07:54.765
Regardless of its exact species,

00:07:54.849 --> 00:07:56.976
Yggdrasil's health is
vital to the health

00:07:57.059 --> 00:08:00.688
of the Norse universe and
to their head god, Odin.

00:08:00.771 --> 00:08:03.774
According to legend, Odin
sacrificed his own blood

00:08:03.858 --> 00:08:07.653
and his eye to himself
by voluntarily hanging

00:08:07.737 --> 00:08:10.698
from Yggdrasil for
nine days, as one does.

00:08:10.781 --> 00:08:13.242
In doing so, he gained
knowledge and wisdom.

00:08:13.326 --> 00:08:15.536
World trees serve
the paramount purpose

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:19.415
of balancing life forces and
supporting a stable universe.

00:08:19.498 --> 00:08:21.709
But if you have more
selfish desires,

00:08:21.792 --> 00:08:23.336
you'll want to look
for a wishing tree.

00:08:23.419 --> 00:08:24.754
Throughout the world,

00:08:24.837 --> 00:08:27.548
ancient and modern people
alike have made wishes on trees

00:08:27.632 --> 00:08:29.425
by tying ribbons
to their branches,

00:08:29.508 --> 00:08:31.677
hammering special
coins into their bark,

00:08:31.761 --> 00:08:33.012
or tossing notes
into their canopies.

00:08:33.095 --> 00:08:36.599
Hindu myths tell of
the Kalpavriksha,

00:08:36.682 --> 00:08:38.184
a wish granting
tree that emerged

00:08:38.267 --> 00:08:39.685
from the primordial waters

00:08:39.769 --> 00:08:42.897
during a legendary battle
between the gods or devas

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:45.024
and demons or asuras.

00:08:45.107 --> 00:08:47.360
After being poisoned,
the devas searched

00:08:47.443 --> 00:08:50.529
for a healing elixir
at the bottom of a milky ocean.

00:08:50.613 --> 00:08:51.948
But in their churning

00:08:52.031 --> 00:08:53.866
brought up many treasures
from the depths.

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:57.161
It's unclear what type of
tree the Kalpavriksha is,

00:08:57.245 --> 00:08:58.829
though many compare
it to the banyan,

00:08:58.913 --> 00:09:01.249
one of India's
most sacred trees.

00:09:01.332 --> 00:09:03.459
Myths like these
make trees sacred,

00:09:03.543 --> 00:09:06.379
or at least worthy of
our care and protection.

00:09:06.462 --> 00:09:08.506
Other stories act
as cautionary tales

00:09:08.589 --> 00:09:11.133
that scare us into looking
after our woody friends.

00:09:11.217 --> 00:09:13.761
Japanese folklore has
tales of the Jubokko,

00:09:13.844 --> 00:09:15.846
a yokai who forms
when enough blood

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:18.391
has been violently
spilled on its roots.

00:09:18.474 --> 00:09:20.893
These trees who have
seen too little kindness

00:09:20.977 --> 00:09:22.728
and received too few thanks

00:09:22.812 --> 00:09:25.273
turn hostile and use
their sharp branches

00:09:25.356 --> 00:09:29.193
to grab anyone who gets too
close and drain their blood.

00:09:29.277 --> 00:09:32.238
Having grown up surrounded
by trees in a lifestyle

00:09:32.321 --> 00:09:35.241
slightly more reminiscent
of our ancestors than most,

00:09:35.324 --> 00:09:37.451
I personally understand
the importance

00:09:37.535 --> 00:09:38.869
of treating them well.

00:09:38.953 --> 00:09:41.205
A sick tree on your
property can fall down

00:09:41.289 --> 00:09:44.166
on your possessions
or spread rot to its neighbors.

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:47.503
Trees become the stuff of
nightmares when we neglect them,

00:09:47.586 --> 00:09:49.046
but they literally keep us alive

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:51.674
when we give them
the bare minimum of care.

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:53.676
Let's all take a hint from
these ancient cultures

00:09:53.759 --> 00:09:56.554
all over the world and
stop every once in a while

00:09:56.637 --> 00:09:59.223
to see trees
for the true gifts they are.

00:09:59.307 --> 00:10:01.434
Clearly having a close
connection with nature

00:10:01.517 --> 00:10:03.311
is something very
personal to me,

00:10:03.394 --> 00:10:05.104
which is why
I'm so excited to tell you

00:10:05.187 --> 00:10:08.816
about a new series on PBS Terra
called "Women of the Earth."

00:10:08.899 --> 00:10:10.776
It's a series that
introduces you to women

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:12.987
who are pioneering
movements to heal the Earth

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:14.447
from climate change.

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:16.073
There is a link in
the description.

00:10:16.157 --> 00:10:17.491
We hope you check it out

00:10:17.575 --> 00:10:20.453
and the rest of PBS's
Earth Month playlist too.

00:10:20.536 --> 00:10:22.538
[no audio]

00:10:22.621 --> 00:10:25.666
Accessibility provided by the
U.S. Department of Education.

00:10:27.835 --> 00:10:30.963
[no audio continues]

